{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.FluidDynamics.q_ns_energy_and_decay","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.q_ns_energy_and_decay","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.ODE.Gronwall"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.energy_inequality_of_balance","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.energy_inequality_of_balance","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/q_ns_energy_and_decay.lean#L72-L107","doc":"**Energy inequality from the pointwise energy balance.**\n\nLet `E` be the squared norm `‖v(t)‖²_A` and `Dcum` the cumulative\ndissipation `t ↦ ∫₀ᵗ ‖d_q v(s)‖²_A ds`.  If the balance\n`E' t + 2 ν_q · D t ≤ 0` holds pointwise — the differential form of\nthe energy identity — then the *integrated* inequality\n\n`E t + 2 ν_q · Dcum t ≤ E 0`\n\nholds on `[0, T]`.  This is property (ii) of\n[`thm:q-ns-wellposedness`].\n\nNote what is and is not assumed: the hypothesis is the pointwise\nbalance (a statement about the equations), and the conclusion is the\nintegrated bound (the statement the block makes).  Assuming the\nlatter directly, as the previous encoding did, assumes the theorem. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.modal_decay_of_deriv_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.modal_decay_of_deriv_le","kind":"theorem","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/q_ns_energy_and_decay.lean#L44-L70","doc":"**Modal exponential decay from the modal differential inequality.**\n\nIf a real trajectory `a` satisfies `a' t ≤ -c · a t` on `[0, T)`,\nthen `a t ≤ a 0 · exp (-c t)` on `[0, T]`.\n\nApplied to the `Δ_q`-eigencomponent `a = |v̂_k|` with\n`c = ν_q [k]_q²`, this is property (iii) of\n[`thm:q-ns-wellposedness`].  The eigenvalue enters only as the\nconstant `c`; nothing here knows about `q`, which is the point —\nthe decay is a consequence of the modal inequality, not an extra\nassumption about solutions. ","signatureChunk":0}]}